{"id":"refine_20260928013408946","summary":"Added a global, task-family-specific heuristic for matching noisy panels to motifs in ARC composition puzzles.","rationale":"In task-9a25a41d, repeated assignments based on raw hole counts and mask overlap failed, while matching by the number of 4-connected components in anomalous cells led to the correct composition. The user explicitly requested preserving this as a heuristic, not a universal rule.","expectedOutcome":"For similar puzzles, the agent will test component-count matching before relying on raw counts or overlap, then validate the proposed composition against examples.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_panel_motif_component_matching_heuristic","title":"ARC panel-and-motif composition: try 4-connected component matching","content":"For ARC tasks with large solid-color panels containing scattered background-color cells and smaller motifs on that same background, consider matching panels to motifs by the number of 4-connected components formed by the anomalous/background cells—not just by hole count or mask overlap. If supported by the examples, preserve the panel layout, place each motif inside a one-cell border of the panel color, and recolor motif-background cells to that panel color. This is a task-family heuristic, not a universal ARC rule; verify it against demonstrations before applying. Evidence: task-9a25a41d’s component-count matching produced the correct composition after several overlap- and count-based assignments failed.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Preserves a reusable, evidence-backed ARC strategy explicitly requested by the user.","after":{"id":"arc_panel_motif_component_matching_heuristic","kind":"memory","title":"ARC panel-and-motif composition: try 4-connected component matching","content":"For ARC tasks with large solid-color panels containing scattered background-color cells and smaller motifs on that same background, consider matching panels to motifs by the number of 4-connected components formed by the anomalous/background cells—not just by hole count or mask overlap. If supported by the examples, preserve the panel layout, place each motif inside a one-cell border of the panel color, and recolor motif-background cells to that panel color. This is a task-family heuristic, not a universal ARC rule; verify it against demonstrations before applying. Evidence: task-9a25a41d’s component-count matching produced the correct composition after several overlap- and count-based assignments failed.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T01:34:13.887Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T01:34:13.887Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545227/attempts/8164bfac1c454b9f9e3e84cc67e27983/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928020029340","summary":"Added a separate global ARC memory for the validated boundary-contour recoloring heuristic.","rationale":"The trajectory solved the test grid correctly after using the three demonstrations to infer a rule distinct from the existing panel-and-motif heuristic.","expectedOutcome":"On similar ARC puzzles, the agent can test this component-and-boundary rule against demonstrations before applying it to a test grid.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_marker_selected_boundary_contour_heuristic","title":"ARC marker-selected boundary contour recoloring","content":"For ARC puzzles matching the demonstrated pattern, where 8 is background, 1 is an obstacle, 6 is a marker, and 7 is the contour color: find the 4-connected component of 8/background containing 6. Preserve all 1s and the 6. Recolor to 7 the cells in that selected component that lie on the grid edge or are 8-neighbor adjacent to a 1-cell in a 4-connected component of 1s that touches the grid boundary. Leave other cells unchanged. This rule matched the three demonstrations and produced the correctly submitted test output for task-5b079f52. Keep this heuristic separate from panel-and-motif component matching; verify the topology and color roles against examples before applying it.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The successful solution and three demonstrations provide evidence for a reusable ARC heuristic with a clear scope and a validation caveat.","after":{"id":"arc_marker_selected_boundary_contour_heuristic","kind":"memory","title":"ARC marker-selected boundary contour recoloring","content":"For ARC puzzles matching the demonstrated pattern, where 8 is background, 1 is an obstacle, 6 is a marker, and 7 is the contour color: find the 4-connected component of 8/background containing 6. Preserve all 1s and the 6. Recolor to 7 the cells in that selected component that lie on the grid edge or are 8-neighbor adjacent to a 1-cell in a 4-connected component of 1s that touches the grid boundary. Leave other cells unchanged. This rule matched the three demonstrations and produced the correctly submitted test output for task-5b079f52. Keep this heuristic separate from panel-and-motif component matching; verify the topology and color roles against examples before applying it.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T02:00:33.560Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T02:00:33.560Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545227/attempts/8164bfac1c454b9f9e3e84cc67e27983/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928040355061","summary":"Added a global memory describing the demonstrated ARC quadrant-pattern transformation, with a caution to verify panel roles and dimensions on examples.","rationale":"The user requested this reusable lesson, and the demos plus solved test support counting nonzero cells in the top panels for output dimensions and using the lower panels to construct a repeated output tile.","expectedOutcome":"For similar four-panel ARC puzzles, the agent can test the transformation against demonstrations before applying it, including cases where top panels are empty or the background convention differs.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_quadrant_pattern_tile_transform","title":"ARC quadrant-pattern tile transform","content":"For ARC puzzles with four 4x4 panels, test this rule against demonstrations: the counts of non-background cells in the top-left and top-right panels may specify output height and width, respectively. Construct the output by repeating the bottom-right 4x4 panel with row/column indices modulo 4. Preserve its non-background cells; at background cells, use the corresponding bottom-left panel cell with the same modulo-4 coordinates. Verify the background color, panel roles, dimension counts, and output rule on examples before applying; empty top panels may follow special behavior.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The user explicitly requested a reusable global memory, and the transformation is supported by the demonstrated examples and the correctly solved test.","after":{"id":"arc_quadrant_pattern_tile_transform","kind":"memory","title":"ARC quadrant-pattern tile transform","content":"For ARC puzzles with four 4x4 panels, test this rule against demonstrations: the counts of non-background cells in the top-left and top-right panels may specify output height and width, respectively. Construct the output by repeating the bottom-right 4x4 panel with row/column indices modulo 4. Preserve its non-background cells; at background cells, use the corresponding bottom-left panel cell with the same modulo-4 coordinates. Verify the background color, panel roles, dimension counts, and output rule on examples before applying; empty top panels may follow special behavior.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T04:03:59.952Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T04:03:59.952Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545227/attempts/8164bfac1c454b9f9e3e84cc67e27983/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928083149966","summary":"Added a global ARC memory for extracting the unique framed panel in each row of a tiled grid.","rationale":"The demonstrated task showed row-wise selection of a panel whose object-cell pattern differed from the other panels, preserving its framing and stacking selected panels vertically; the successful test supports applying this rule when the demonstrations establish it.","expectedOutcome":"On similar tiled-panel ARC tasks, the agent can test exact pattern differences within each panel row, extract the demonstrated outlier, and verify output dimensions and framing against examples.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_framed_panel_rowwise_outlier_extraction","title":"ARC framed-panel row-wise outlier extraction","content":"For ARC puzzles with grids tiled into framed panels, test whether demonstrations show one panel per panel row being selected because its object-cell pattern differs from the others in that row. Compare panel interiors by exact cell pattern, extract the demonstrated unique outlier from each row, and preserve its framing/background when stacking the selected panels vertically. Validate panel dimensions, output boundaries, and that each selected panel is the row-wise outlier before applying the rule to the test grid.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"A demo established row-wise outlier extraction with preserved panel framing, and applying the corresponding extraction to the test grid produced a correct submission.","after":{"id":"arc_framed_panel_rowwise_outlier_extraction","kind":"memory","title":"ARC framed-panel row-wise outlier extraction","content":"For ARC puzzles with grids tiled into framed panels, test whether demonstrations show one panel per panel row being selected because its object-cell pattern differs from the others in that row. Compare panel interiors by exact cell pattern, extract the demonstrated unique outlier from each row, and preserve its framing/background when stacking the selected panels vertically. Validate panel dimensions, output boundaries, and that each selected panel is the row-wise outlier before applying the rule to the test grid.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T08:31:57.806Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T08:31:57.806Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545227/attempts/8164bfac1c454b9f9e3e84cc67e27983/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928090612571","summary":"Added a global memory for the demonstrated ARC rule that turns 1/2 bars into a 3-colored serpentine contour.","rationale":"The task was solved after five demonstrations and one incorrect submission; the successful solution recolored 1s to 2s and corrected the contour placement using the bar’s 1-count-derived offset.","expectedOutcome":"On similar marker-and-bar puzzles, test this transformation against demonstrations, carefully derive each rail’s offset and connection direction from the marker, and avoid estimating the rail from a neighboring bar’s midpoint.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_marker_guided_bar_serpentine_contour","title":"ARC marker-guided serpentine contour around 1/2 bars","content":"For ARC puzzles with background 8, a single 3 marker, and straight bars made of 1s and 2s, test this rule against the demonstrations: recolor all 1 cells to 2, then draw a 3-colored serpentine contour on the marker-facing side of the bars. For each bar, let k be its count of 1 cells; the contour rail is offset by k+1 from the relevant bar boundary, extends along the bar axis by the corresponding offset, and connects around successive bars. Derive the correct boundary, endpoint, and turn direction from the marker’s position and the demonstrations rather than assuming a midpoint. In the validated instance, the left contour boundary was the upper bar’s start minus (k+1), not a midpoint inferred from the lower bar. Preserve existing 2s and the marker; verify the complete path on all examples before applying it.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The successful solution validated a reusable marker-and-bar contour pattern and exposed a specific rail-placement error that is useful to guard against in future similar ARC tasks.","after":{"id":"arc_marker_guided_bar_serpentine_contour","kind":"memory","title":"ARC marker-guided serpentine contour around 1/2 bars","content":"For ARC puzzles with background 8, a single 3 marker, and straight bars made of 1s and 2s, test this rule against the demonstrations: recolor all 1 cells to 2, then draw a 3-colored serpentine contour on the marker-facing side of the bars. For each bar, let k be its count of 1 cells; the contour rail is offset by k+1 from the relevant bar boundary, extends along the bar axis by the corresponding offset, and connects around successive bars. Derive the correct boundary, endpoint, and turn direction from the marker’s position and the demonstrations rather than assuming a midpoint. In the validated instance, the left contour boundary was the upper bar’s start minus (k+1), not a midpoint inferred from the lower bar. Preserve existing 2s and the marker; verify the complete path on all examples before applying it.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T09:06:19.714Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T09:06:19.714Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545227/attempts/8164bfac1c454b9f9e3e84cc67e27983/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928092233354","summary":"Added a global ARC memory for testing corner-marker-driven nested patterns in panels separated by divider lines.","rationale":"The demonstrations support using marker colors and corner orientation to construct patterns within separated panels, but the first test submission was wrong and the corrected submission only validates a solid fill for the single-marker lower-left panel. The heuristic therefore needs explicit demonstration-based validation rather than treating alternating bands as universal.","expectedOutcome":"For similar ARC tasks, the agent will test panel bounds, divider preservation, and marker orientation against every demonstration, and avoid assuming that a single marker implies alternating bands.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_divided_panels_corner_marker_nested_patterns","title":"ARC divided panels with corner-marker patterns","content":"For ARC grids partitioned by straight divider lines, test whether individual panels are filled with nested rectangular bands or other patterns determined by colored cells placed diagonally from a panel corner. Use demonstrations to infer panel bounds, corner orientation, and the marker-to-band color sequence; preserve divider lines. Do not assume one marker produces alternating bands: in the demonstrated task, a panel with a single marker was filled uniformly with that marker's color. Leave unmarked panels unchanged as the background only if examples support that behavior. Validate the complete proposed transformation against all examples before submission.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Preserves the reusable panel-and-marker hypothesis while incorporating the observed single-marker solid-fill case and guarding against the failed overgeneralization.","after":{"id":"arc_divided_panels_corner_marker_nested_patterns","kind":"memory","title":"ARC divided panels with corner-marker patterns","content":"For ARC grids partitioned by straight divider lines, test whether individual panels are filled with nested rectangular bands or other patterns determined by colored cells placed diagonally from a panel corner. Use demonstrations to infer panel bounds, corner orientation, and the marker-to-band color sequence; preserve divider lines. Do not assume one marker produces alternating bands: in the demonstrated task, a panel with a single marker was filled uniformly with that marker's color. Leave unmarked panels unchanged as the background only if examples support that behavior. Validate the complete proposed transformation against all examples before submission.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T09:22:40.224Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T09:22:40.224Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545227/attempts/8164bfac1c454b9f9e3e84cc67e27983/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928094936796","summary":"Added a global ARC memory for arranging hollow square frames and solid square blocks into paired output bands.","rationale":"The demonstrated task was solved after correctly distinguishing hollow frames from filled blocks, ordering each category by reading order, and padding the shorter category with background.","expectedOutcome":"For similar ARC tasks, the agent can test this arrangement against demonstrations and avoid shape-classification, ordering, and output-dimension errors.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_hollow_frame_solid_block_pairing","title":"ARC hollow-frame and solid-block pairing","content":"For ARC puzzles containing equal-size hollow square frames and solid square blocks, test whether the output arranges the two categories in adjacent columns, one object per horizontal band. Classify hollow frames by their empty interiors and solid blocks by their filled interiors; do not mistake frames for solid blocks. Order each category independently by reading order (top-to-bottom, then left-to-right). Each band places the next frame on the left and the next solid block on the right. If the categories have different counts, pad the shorter column with background; the number of bands is the larger category count. In the demonstrated task, frames 9, 7, 8 paired in order with solid blocks 6, 1, 2, 4, leaving the final left slot as background. On the test, frames 1, 9, 4 paired with blocks 7, 8, leaving the final right slot as background. Verify object dimensions, ordering, padding, and output dimensions against all demonstrations before applying.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"This is a reusable, demonstration-supported ARC transformation; the initially incorrect attempt illustrates why accurate hollow-versus-solid classification and independent ordering matter.","after":{"id":"arc_hollow_frame_solid_block_pairing","kind":"memory","title":"ARC hollow-frame and solid-block pairing","content":"For ARC puzzles containing equal-size hollow square frames and solid square blocks, test whether the output arranges the two categories in adjacent columns, one object per horizontal band. Classify hollow frames by their empty interiors and solid blocks by their filled interiors; do not mistake frames for solid blocks. Order each category independently by reading order (top-to-bottom, then left-to-right). Each band places the next frame on the left and the next solid block on the right. If the categories have different counts, pad the shorter column with background; the number of bands is the larger category count. In the demonstrated task, frames 9, 7, 8 paired in order with solid blocks 6, 1, 2, 4, leaving the final left slot as background. On the test, frames 1, 9, 4 paired with blocks 7, 8, leaving the final right slot as background. Verify object dimensions, ordering, padding, and output dimensions against all demonstrations before applying.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T09:49:43.072Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T09:49:43.072Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545227/attempts/8164bfac1c454b9f9e3e84cc67e27983/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928115342801","summary":"Added a global ARC memory for filling regions defined by overlapping spans of parallel diagonal marker lines.","rationale":"The demos established the fill rule, and the first submission using only adjacent marker-line pairs was incorrect; testing every pair produced the correct output.","expectedOutcome":"On similar ARC grids, the agent can test the all-pairs diagonal-span rule against demonstrations and avoid missing fill regions defined by non-adjacent lines.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_parallel_diagonal_marker_pairwise_span_fill","title":"ARC fill between parallel diagonal marker lines using all pairs","content":"For ARC puzzles matching the demonstrated pattern—background 8, parallel down-right diagonal marker lines of one color (such as 9), and fill color 2—test this rule against the demonstrations. Represent each marker line by its offset d=c-r and the interval of s=r+c values at its endpoints. For every pair of marker lines, including non-adjacent pairs, fill background cells with 2 when their d lies strictly between the two offsets and their s lies in the intersection of the two lines’ endpoint intervals. Preserve all marker cells and other non-background cells. Testing only adjacent pairs can miss fill regions; validate the all-pairs rule on every demonstration before applying it.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The trajectory verified this reusable geometric rule: an adjacent-pairs-only fill was incorrect, while the all-pairs method solved the task.","after":{"id":"arc_parallel_diagonal_marker_pairwise_span_fill","kind":"memory","title":"ARC fill between parallel diagonal marker lines using all pairs","content":"For ARC puzzles matching the demonstrated pattern—background 8, parallel down-right diagonal marker lines of one color (such as 9), and fill color 2—test this rule against the demonstrations. Represent each marker line by its offset d=c-r and the interval of s=r+c values at its endpoints. For every pair of marker lines, including non-adjacent pairs, fill background cells with 2 when their d lies strictly between the two offsets and their s lies in the intersection of the two lines’ endpoint intervals. Preserve all marker cells and other non-background cells. Testing only adjacent pairs can miss fill regions; validate the all-pairs rule on every demonstration before applying it.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T11:53:47.414Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T11:53:47.415Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545227/attempts/8164bfac1c454b9f9e3e84cc67e27983/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260928162440175","summary":"Added a global ARC memory for matching small colored 3×3 motifs to solid panels and carving the motif into each panel.","rationale":"The task’s demonstration and successful solution support a reusable rule: for each panel color, use the same-color small motif as a mask, turning occupied motif cells into background holes while leaving blank motif cells in the panel color. The first incorrect submission reversed this polarity, so the memory emphasizes validating filled-versus-hole polarity against demonstrations.","expectedOutcome":"On similar ARC tasks, the agent can test the color-matched carving rule, preserve panel order and spacing, and verify the output layout and mask polarity against examples.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_color_matched_motif_panel_carving","title":"ARC color-matched motif carving in solid panels","content":"For ARC puzzles with small 3×3 colored motifs and larger solid square panels in matching colors, test whether each panel is transformed using the same-color motif as a mask: change panel cells at occupied motif positions to the background, leaving cells at blank motif positions in the panel color. Validate the polarity against every demonstration; filled-versus-hole polarity is easy to reverse. Preserve the panels’ relative arrangement and intervening background gaps, and crop the output to the panels with a one-cell background margin when the examples support that layout.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"A solved task demonstrated this transformation, and an incorrect attempt reversed the motif’s filled-versus-hole polarity before the demonstrations clarified the rule.","after":{"id":"arc_color_matched_motif_panel_carving","kind":"memory","title":"ARC color-matched motif carving in solid panels","content":"For ARC puzzles with small 3×3 colored motifs and larger solid square panels in matching colors, test whether each panel is transformed using the same-color motif as a mask: change panel cells at occupied motif positions to the background, leaving cells at blank motif positions in the panel color. Validate the polarity against every demonstration; filled-versus-hole polarity is easy to reverse. Preserve the panels’ relative arrangement and intervening background gaps, and crop the output to the panels with a one-cell background margin when the examples support that layout.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-28T16:24:45.309Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T16:24:45.309Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790545227/attempts/8164bfac1c454b9f9e3e84cc67e27983/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
